        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/1670f48c1bd70b2c5ddee70c4a9c893edf5e844febd7128b1ddf7773aef0f73f.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-1.$ Find the vector $Cv.$",
        "worked_solution": "The eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cv & =-1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/2e9b0004ae889fb63f5ae65bd2a79915b6e195935fd3b610d2f9957d10f6a9e7.png)",
